h>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>125280 / 1</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>3/30/2009</td>\n<td>0.5 ML ( 0.25 OR 0.5 ML/DOSE )<br><br>SUSPENSION / INTRAMUSCULAR / PREFILLED SYRINGE</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n\nNovember 2026  \nPage 71 of 73--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSeptember 17, 2026 Approval Letter - FAYUVI (https://www.fda.gov/media/194921/download)\nciteturn186search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 3 days ago; **BLA APPROVAL**  \nSeptember 17, 2026 ... You may label your product with the proprietary name FAYUVII and market it in single-dose vials containing 2.0 mL extractable volume at a concentration of 1.0 × 10¹³ vector genomes/mL (vg/mL). ... www.fda.gov\nU.S. FOOD & DRUG ADMINISTRATION\n\nOur STN: BL 125845/0\n\n**BLA APPROVAL**  \nSeptember 17, 2026\n\nUltragenyx Pharmaceutical Inc.  \nAttention: Jad Adaimi  \n5000 Marina Blvd.  \nBrisbane, CA 94005\n\nDear Jad Adaimi:\n\nPlease refer to your Biologics License Application (BLA) received December 18, 2024, submitted under section 351(a) of the Public Health Service Act (PHS Act) for rebisufligene etisparvovec-hopf.\n\nWe acknowledge receipt of your amendment dated March 20, 2026, which constituted a complete response to our July 7, 2025, action letter.\n\n**LICENSING**\n\nWe have approved your BLA for rebisufligene etisparvovec-hopf effective on this date. You are hereby authorized to introduce or deliver for introduction into interstate commerce, rebisufligene etisparvovec-hopf under your existing Department of Health and Human Services U.S. License No. 2040. Rebisufligene etisparvovec-hopf is indicated for the treatment of neurologic manifestations of mucopolysaccharidosis type IIIA (MPS IIIA, Sanfilippo Syndrome type A) in pediatric patients with preserved neurodevelopmental function.\n\nThe review of this product was associated with the following National Clinical Trial (NCT) number(s): NCT02716246, NCT04360265, and NCT04088734.\n\n**MANUFACTURING LOCATIONS**\n\nUnder this license, you are approved to manufacture rebisufligene etisparvovec-hopf drug substance at Andelyn Biosciences, Inc., 1180 Arthur E Adams Dr., Columbus, OH 43221. The final formulated product will be manufactured and filled at Ultragenyx Pharmaceutical Inc., 170 Middlesex Turnpike, Bedford, MA 01730 and labeled and packaged at (b) (4).\n\nYou may label your product with the proprietary name FAYUVII and market it in single-dose vials containing 2.0 mL extractable volume at a concentration of 1.0 × 10¹³ vector genomes/mL (vg/mL).\n\n---\n\nU.S. Food & Drug Administration  \n10903 New Hampshire Avenue  \nSilver Spring, MD 20993  \nwww.fda.gov--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFederal Register / Vol. 91, No. 192 / Tuesday, October 6, 2026 / Notices 63569 (https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-10-06/pdf/2026-20424.pdf)\nciteturn186search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 3 days ago; Food and Drug Administration guidance for industry “E6(R3) Good Clinical Practice” (September 2025), available at https://www.fda.gov/media/169090/download. ... FDA has determined that FAYUVI (rebisufligene etisparvovec-hopf), approved on September 17, 2026, manufactured by Ultragenyx Pharmaceutical Inc., meets the criteria for a priority review voucher.\nBILLING CODE 4164–01–P\n\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES\n\nFood and Drug Administration\n\n[Docket No. FDA–2026–N–11259]\n\nIssuance of Priority Review Voucher; Rare Pediatric Disease Product; FAYUVI (rebisufligene etisparvovec-hopf)\n\nAGENCY: Food and Drug Administration, HHS.  \nACTION: Notice.\n\nSUMMARY: The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is announcing the issuance of a priority review voucher to the sponsor of a rare pediatric disease product application. The Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act (FD&C Act) authorizes FDA to award priority review vouchers to sponsors of approved rare pediatric disease product applications that meet certain criteria. FDA is required to publish notice of the award of the priority review voucher. FDA has determined that FAYUVI (rebisufligene etisparvovec-hopf), approved on September 17, 2026, manufactured by Ultragenyx Pharmaceutical Inc., meets the criteria for a priority review voucher.\n\nFOR FURTHER INFORMATION CONTACT: Myrna Hanna, Center for Biologics Evaluation and Research, Food and Drug Administration, *industry.biologics@fda.hhs.gov*, 240–402–7911.\n\nSUPPLEMENTARY INFORMATION: FDA is announcing the issuance of a priority review voucher to the sponsor of an approved rare pediatric disease product application. Under section 529 of the FD&C Act (21 U.S.C. 360ff), FDA will award priority review vouchers to sponsors of approved rare pediatric disease product applications that meet certain criteria. FDA has determined that FAYUVI (rebisufligene etisparvovec-hopf), manufactured by Ultragenyx Pharmaceutical Inc., meets the criteria for a priority review voucher. FAYUVI (rebisufligene etisparvovec-hopf) is an adeno-associated virus (AAV) vector-based gene therapy indicated for the treatment of neurologic manifestations of mucopolysaccharidosis type IIIA (MPS IIIA, Sanfilippo syndrome type A) in pediatric patients with preserved neurodevelopmental function.\n\nFor further information about the Rare Pediatric Disease Priority Review Voucher Program and for a link to the full text of section 529 of the FD&C Act, go to https://www.fda.gov/industry/developing-products-rare-diseases-conditions/rare-pediatric-disease-rpd-designation-and-voucher-programs. For further information about FAYUVI (rebisufligene etisparvovec-hopf), go to the Center for Biologics Evaluation and Research’s Approved Cellular and Gene Therapy Products website at https://www.fda.gov/vaccines-blood-biologics/cellular-gene-therapy-products/approved-cellular-and-gene-therapy-products.\n\nGrace R. Graham,  \n*Deputy Commissioner for Policy, Legislation, and International Affairs.*  \n[FR Doc. 2026–20434 Filed 10–5–26; 8:45 am]  \nBILLING CODE 4164–01–P\n\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES\n\nNational Institutes of Health\n\nNational Cancer Institute; Notice of Meeting\n\nPursuant to section 1009 of the Federal Advisory Committee Act, as amended, notice is hereby given of a meeting of the President’s Cancer Panel.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSeptember 17, 2026 Summary Basis for Regulatory Action - FAYUVI (https://www.fda.gov/media/195198/download)\nciteturn186search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 3 days ago; 6. Allowing product approval without contemporaneous authorization of an *in vitro* companion diagnostic device to detect anti-AAV9 antibodies to determine treatment eligibility, with a post-marketing commitment (PMC) to validate a companion anti-AAV9 antibody diagnostic assay and update the labeling accordingly. ... In addition to the proposed pharmacovigilance, the applicant will conduct an international observational study (MPS IIIA Disease Monitoring Plan or Protocol number UX111-CL401) that plans to enroll pediatric patients who have completed FAYUVI clinical trials as well as newly treated patients in the post-marketing setting. ... Conduct adequate analytical and clinical validation studies to support revisions to the labeling of FAYUVI to specify the use of an FDA-authorized *in vitro* companion diagnostic device to accurately and reliably identify patients who do not have anti-AAV9 Antibodies ≥1:100 and for whom FAYUVI is indicated.\n6. Allowing product approval without contemporaneous authorization of an *in vitro* companion diagnostic device to detect anti-AAV9 antibodies to determine treatment eligibility, with a post-marketing commitment (PMC) to validate a companion anti-AAV9 antibody diagnostic assay and update the labeling accordingly.\n\nThe totality of evidence supports a favorable benefit-risk assessment for the mITT population studied in the Applicant’s clinical development program (i.e., early-stage MPS IIIA). Currently available clinical outcome data for patients with more advanced disease at the time of treatment are inadequate to support a clear treatment benefit in this advanced stage of disease. Additional, more interpretable, clinical outcome data for patients who are in more advanced stages of disease at the time of treatment are likely needed to support approval for this subpopulation.\n\n### c. Recommendation for Post-marketing Activities\n\nThe observed safety profile for FAYUVI is consistent with the known class effects of AAV gene therapy products, making the risks manageable through product labeling and the proposed pharmacovigilance plan. There were no safety signals that necessitate a REMS or required post-marketing study designed to evaluate a particular safety issue.\n\nIn addition to the proposed pharmacovigilance, the applicant will conduct an international observational study (MPS IIIA Disease Monitoring Plan or Protocol number UX111-CL401) that plans to enroll pediatric patients who have completed FAYUVI clinical trials as well as newly treated patients in the post-marketing setting. The study will follow patients for at least 10 years after FAYUVI administration and will focus on long-term safety and effectiveness in the real-world post-marketing setting.\n\nThe following Post-marketing Commitments were agreed upon by Ultragenyx Pharmaceutical Inc:\n\n1. Conduct adequate analytical and clinical validation studies to support revisions to the labeling of FAYUVI to specify the use of an FDA-authorized *in vitro* companion diagnostic device to accurately and reliably identify patients who do not have anti-AAV9 Antibodies ≥1:100 and for whom FAYUVI is indicated.\n\n   The final study report should be accompanied by a revised labeling Prior Approval Supplement incorporating the authorized companion diagnostic device information.\n\n   **Study Completion:** December 31, 2028  \n   **Final Report Submission:** June 30, 2029\n\n2. Ultragenyx commits to re-evaluating the acceptance criteria for the [redacted] (b)(4) QC release tests performed on the drug substance and drug product. Ultragenyx will submit the test results and, if applicable, revise the acceptance limits and justifications. The study report should be submitted within 60 days after the 15th commercial lot is released under the US license as a Postmarketing Study Commitment\n\n20--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUpdate on UK process for exploring international access to FAYUVI | MPS Society (https://prod.mpssociety.org.uk/latest/update-on-uk-process-for-exploring-international-access-to-fayuvi)\nciteturn186search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: today; On 17 September 2026, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved FAYUVI (rebisufligene etisparvovec-hopf), previously known as UX111, for paediatric patients with MPS IIIA (Sanfilippo syndrome type A). ... FDA approval states that individuals under the age of 18 with preserved neurodevelopmental function may be eligible for treatment.\n\n  1.   2. Latest\n\n  3. Update on UK process for exploring international access to FAYUVI\n\nImage: .\n\nCondition update published on 7 October 2026\n\n# Update on UK process for exploring international access to FAYUVI\n\nOn 17 September 2026, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved FAYUVI (rebisufligene etisparvovec-hopf), previously known as UX111, for paediatric patients with MPS IIIA (Sanfilippo syndrome type A). This is the first approved therapy intended to address the underlying cause of MPS IIIA.\n\nFAYUVI is a one-time intravenous gene therapy that uses an AAV9 viral vector to deliver a functioning copy of the SGSH gene, enabling production of the missing sulfamidase enzyme.\n\nThis approval represents a significant milestone for the global Sanfilippo community. We recognise the urgency within our MPS IIIA community to understand what this approval means for them and how they might explore accessing the therapy.\n\nThis guide is intended to help UK families understand the steps involved if they wish to explore accessing FAYUVI in the United States.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2026 Biological License Application Approvals | FDA (https://www.fda.gov/vaccines-blood-biologics/development-approval-process-cber/2026-biological-license-application-approvals)\nciteturn186search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: today; # 2026 Biological License Application Approvals ... Fayuvi rebisufligene etisparvovec-hopf  | Indicated for the treatment of neurologic manifestations of mucopolysaccharidosis type IIIA (MPS IIIA, Sanfilippo Syndrome type A) in pediatric patients with preserved neurodevelopmental function. ... The indication for persons 65 years of age and older is approved under accelerated approval pursuant to section 506(c) of the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act (FDCA) and the regulations for accelerated approval, 21 CFR 601.41.\n\n# 2026 Biological License Application Approvals\n\nThis list reflects information regarding the applications as of the approval date. It is not updated with regard to applicant or application status changes. The applications are listed by date of approval.\n\n2026 Biological License Application Approvals\nTradename/Proper Name       | Indication for Use  | STN  | Manufacturer/\nLicense Number  | Approval Date\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nFayuvirebisufligene etisparvovec-hopf  | Indicated for the treatment of neurologic manifestations of mucopolysaccharidosis type IIIA (MPS IIIA, Sanfilippo Syndrome type A) in pediatric patients with preserved neurodevelopmental function.  | 125845/0  | Ultragenyx Pharmaceutical Inc.5000 Marina Blvd.\nBrisbane, CA 94005 Lic. # 2040  | 9/17/2026\n \nGENGLYCOSpariglasgene brecaparvovec-opnr  | Indicated to reduce daily cornstarch intake as an adjunct to nutritional management in adult and pediatric patients 8 years of age and older with glycogen storage disease type Ia (GSDIa).  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSOBI003 Safe, Reduces Heparan Sulfate Levels in 1st Trial in Humans | Development of Experimental Treatment Has Been Discontinued | Sanfilippo Syndrome News (https://sanfilipponews.com/news/sobi003-safe-reduces-heparan-sulfate-levels-in-1st-trial-humans-experimental-treatment/)\nciteturn186search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.3 years ago; Crawled: last month;   *   *   *   * Treatment with SOBI003, a now-discontinued experimental therapy for Sanfilippo syndrome type A, was generally well-tolerated and reduced heparan sulfate levels in a clinical trial. ... Sobi was developing the therapy, but the company announced in 2021 that it was discontinuing development.\n\n# SOBI003 Safe, Reduces Heparan Sulfate Levels in 1st Trial in Humans\n\nExperimental Sanfilippo treatment's development stopped for business reasons\n\nWritten by Marisa Horak, MS | Aug. 18, 2022\n\n  *   Discuss\n  *   Print\n  *   Preferred Source on Google\n\nImage: An illustration of a checklist with \n\n  *   *   *   * Treatment with SOBI003, a now-discontinued experimental therapy for Sanfilippo syndrome type A, was generally well-tolerated and reduced heparan sulfate levels in a clinical trial.\n\n“These are very important findings that could inform future trials and therapeutic development programs targeted at reduction of heparan sulfate. They also provide important information in considering the use of [heparan sulfate] as a surrogate biomarker in Sanfilippo syndrome,” Cara O’Neill, chief science officer at the Cure Sanfilippo Foundation, said in a press release.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSCGE Platform (https://scge.mcw.edu/platform/data/report/clinicalTrials/NCT02716246)\nciteturn186search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Drug Product Na